# venvs
backend/.venv/
frontend/.venv/

# Python cache / compiled
**/__pycache__/
**/*.pyc

# Streamlit outputs
frontend/runs/
frontend/runs_uploads/
frontend/.streamlit/

# Preprocessed data
data_preprocessed/

# Local path cache
frontend/.tg_paths.json

# Daily forecast run outputs (artifacts, incl. treasury outputs — never commit).
# Note the pattern: the CONTENTS are ignored, not the directory. `backend/forecast_runs/` would
# exclude the directory itself, and git never descends into an excluded directory -- so the
# negations below would silently do nothing.
backend/forecast_runs/**
!backend/forecast_runs/
!backend/forecast_runs/*/

# ...except the two SUMMARY files. The Agent's entry point is SUMMARY.json, and with the whole
# directory ignored NO run artifact existed in a clone at all -- so a consumer could not read one,
# and the two fields it needs (data_file, client_framing) had nowhere to appear. Fields being
# correct in the writer while absent from every artifact was the blocker, not the code.
#
# Safe to track because these two are AGGREGATE ONLY: model names, MAEs, skill percentages, gate
# verdicts and the data file's NAME. No row-level predictions, no Treasury series. The row-level
# artifacts (predictions_long.csv, leaderboard.csv, metrics_long.csv) stay ignored -- as does
# forecasts/published/, which used to be cited here as the same class and is not: see below.
!backend/forecast_runs/*/SUMMARY.json
!backend/forecast_runs/*/SUMMARY.txt

# OS cruft
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
# Published forecasts are NO LONGER tracked. This reverses the rule that stood here until the
# 2026-08-14 sanitization, and the reversal is deliberate, so the old reasoning is kept:
#
#   "Published forecasts ARE tracked -- forecasts/published/ is the only record of what was
#    actually said, and a scorecard nobody can audit is worthless."
#
# That argument was about auditability and it is still correct. What it missed is WHAT an issue
# contains. forecast.csv carries row-level Treasury figures -- origin_value and the P10/P50/P90
# path for every target-date -- so it is client data, not the aggregate class that
# backend/forecast_runs/*/SUMMARY.json belongs to.
#
# The estimator carve-out that used to sit here had already conceded exactly this point, and it
# is worth reading as the precedent it was. It diverges from the old CSV rule for the strongest
# version of the reason:
#
#   "A fitted estimator embeds client data -- the full training target and trailing-level
#    series, plus tree split thresholds and leaf values that are functions of the training data
#    and CANNOT be scrubbed at all. Committing one would place raw Treasury figures permanently
#    in every clone of a World Bank repository, unremovable without rewriting history."
#
# That was right, and it was drawn too narrow: the numbers those blobs re-derive were sitting
# unprotected in forecast.csv beside them. The whole issue directory now goes, estimators and
# manifest with it, so the carve-out is absorbed rather than repealed -- and if this rule is ever
# revisited, the blobs must stay ignored on their own merits regardless of what is decided about
# the CSV.
#
# The durable record moves to private_vault/published/, which holds every issue. Losing the
# in-clone record is a real cost and it is the smaller one: an auditor with vault access can
# still reconcile every verdict, and no clone of a World Bank repository carries Treasury rows.
forecasts/published/
#
# scorecard.csv stays tracked -- it is header-only until truth arrives. NOTE: its row schema
# includes p10/p50/p90 and y_true, so the first scored row makes it client data too. Revisit
# this line before the data file moves past the earliest published target date.
!forecasts/scorecard.csv

# Phase 2 experiments, also no longer tracked. The previous rule, kept because it names a real
# consequence that is now unavoidable:
#
#   "The append-only log AND its per-run detail JSONs are tracked: verify_log_integrity()
#    requires a JSON for every row, so ignoring runs/ would make a fresh clone fail its own
#    integrity check and 'reproducible from a logged run' a claim with nothing behind it."
#
# Still true, and accepted: the 153 genuine run records are measurements OF client data and were
# moved to private_vault/experiments/ by the sanitization. A clone cannot verify the log because
# a clone does not have the log. It is either that or ship the measurements, and shipping them is
# not available. The vault copy is what verify_log_integrity() runs against.
#
# These two were left untracked-but-not-ignored after the restore, which is the worst of both:
# `git add -A` would have committed them. Named explicitly so it cannot.
experiments/log.csv
experiments/runs/
#
# test_access.log is a local ledger of holdout reads -- it must not be rewritable by a
# checkout, so it stays untracked and §0a of reports/HANDOFF.md is the durable record.
# (Also covered by the *.log rule at the foot of this file; kept for the reason.)
experiments/test_access.log

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# LOCAL-ONLY VAULT FOR REAL CLIENT DATA AND THE REAL AUDIT TRAIL
#
# The sanitization (docs/sessions/2026-08-13-sanitize-and-synthesize.md) replaced the real
# Georgian Treasury dataset with a synthetic one so the repo carries no client data. The real
# files -- and, importantly, the REAL experiments/log.csv with its 153 genuine run records --
# are moved here rather than deleted, because the log is provenance: it is the evidence behind
# every measured claim in reports/ and docs/, and destroying it would make those claims
# unverifiable.
#
# Nothing in here may ever be committed. It is deliberately a single top-level directory so the
# rule is one line and impossible to misread.
private_vault/
data/
.env
backend/outputs/
*.log
